.TH FSFORK 1 2026-10-28 "agentutils 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fsfork \- fork a directory: make a copy-on-write clone of it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fsfork
.RB [ \-\-no\-ignore ]
.RB [ \-\-print ]
.RI [ dir
.RI [ fork ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fsfork
makes a copy-on-write clone of
.IR dir ,
or of the working directory if none is given: the fork.
.PP
On a terminal, it says on standard error where the fork is, and becomes your
shell
.RB ( $SHELL ,
or
.I /bin/sh
if unset) in the fork; exit the shell to come back.
If standard input or standard output is not a terminal, or with
.BR \-\-print ,
it prints the fork's path instead, and nothing else, so that
.B cd $(fsfork)
goes there.
A script run from a terminal is on that terminal too, so it should capture the
path or give
.BR \-\-print .
.PP
The fork is the directory as it is, hidden files, the git repository, and
uncommitted changes included, but for what
.I .gitignore
files ignore and
.BR git (1)
does not track: build output and dependencies, which can be many files and
so slow to fork, and files such as
.IR .env .
So builds in the fork start cold, unless
.B \-\-no\-ignore
is given.
Making it is quick, and it takes no space until either it or the directory
is written to.
From then on, the two are separate: what changes in one does not change in
the other.
.PP
The fork goes in
.IR fork ,
which must not exist, or, if none is given, next to the directory, named
after it and the first number whose fork and base are both free:
.B fsfork
in
.I ~/src/turso
makes
.IR ~/src/turso.1 ,
then
.IR ~/src/turso.2 ,
and so on.
.PP
.B fsfork
also makes the fork's base: another clone of the directory, next to the
fork and named after it,
.I .turso.1.base
for
.IR turso.1 ,
which keeps what the directory was when it was forked.
.BR fsdiff (1)
compares the fork with it, to show what changed in the fork, and only
there.
The fork is cloned from the base, so that the two are the same whatever
changes in the directory meanwhile.
.PP
.B fsfork
keeps no other record of its forks: a fork is a directory, and
.BR ls (1)
lists them,
.BR rm (1)
removes them.
Remove a fork's base with it, or its number stays taken.
.PP
.B fsfork
goes by
.I .gitignore
files, the ones above
.I dir
in its repository too, but not
.I .git/info/exclude
or a global excludes file.
In a directory that is ignored itself, it goes by the ones in the directory.
It runs
.BR git (1)
without the repository's configuration, so that nothing in
.I dir
can make it run a command.
If git is not there, or cannot tell what is ignored,
.B fsfork
says so on standard error, and forks everything.
.PP
In a subdirectory of a repository, it forks the subdirectory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ignore
Fork what is ignored too, so that builds in the fork start warm.
Making it takes longer if what is ignored holds many files.
.TP
.B \-\-print
Print the fork's path, even on a terminal, rather than start a shell there.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if the directory was forked and its path printed, and 1 otherwise, having
made neither the fork nor the base.
On a terminal, the status of the shell, or 1, having made neither, if the shell
cannot be run.
.SH EXAMPLES
Fork the working directory, and work in the fork in a shell of its own:
.PP
.RS
.B fsfork
.RE
.PP
Fork the working directory, and go there in this shell:
.PP
.RS
.B cd $(fsfork)
.RE
.PP
Fork
.I turso
to
.IR parser ,
with its base in
.IR .parser.base :
.PP
.RS
.B fsfork \-\-print turso parser
.RE
.PP
List the forks of
.IR turso ,
and remove one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B ls \-d turso.*
.B rm \-rf turso.1 .turso.1.base
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fork the working directory with its build output, and go there:
.PP
.RS
.B cd $(fsfork \-\-no\-ignore)
.RE
.SH CAVEATS
On macOS, the fork is made with
.BR clonefile (2),
which needs APFS, and the fork on the same volume as the directory.
On Linux, it is made with
.B cp \-\-reflink=auto
and takes no space only on a file system that can share blocks between
files, such as Btrfs or XFS; elsewhere, it is a full copy.
.PP
Hard links in the directory are not hard links in the fork.
.PP
A directory with something ignored in it is made anew, with its permissions
and modification time, but not its other attributes, and on macOS, sockets
and devices beside what is ignored are left out.
.PP
The fork is at another path than the directory, so tools that remember
absolute paths, such as build caches, may rebuild in it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR confine (1),
.BR fsdiff (1),
.BR git (1),
.BR clonefile (2)
